{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the J1 design note at head 40256d33, for request 7d6ce0f6 and review request 0add7e69. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, on the note. The design holds: one table in the membership object, the exact body, the same member reads, the scope's answer ends the copy. Six statements or gaps need fixing before you build. Nothing has run, and I read this against main at b686862e.\n\n## Changes I request\n\n1. Section 2 overclaims. \"An admin, a controller, a checker and another member read nothing\" is false for an admin. `invite-key` takes any active member, and a holder of `membership.invite` can add a key to Ana's member, enrol it and read Ana's copies. Section 6 says this outright for the lost-device case. That authority isn't new, since the same key can sign as Ana, and the act is in the journal. Say so: no role reads a copy directly, a holder of `membership.invite` can reach one by adding a key to that member, and the journal shows that act.\n\n2. The seven-day bound isn't a bound as written. Rows go \"seven days past `notAfter`\", and only a request to these routes deletes them, with no alarm. In an idle room a refused or unlanded request rests at the host for as long as nobody calls. Section 4 names this as J1's one new exposure, so the number has to hold. Use the object's alarm, or state that seven days is the earliest deletion and that no upper bound exists. I prefer the alarm.\n\n3. Define what an in-flight row means. A copy is open from before the send until the answer, and \"while a copy is open at a scope, the page offers only the button\". So a second tab or device, reading during an ordinary act, sees \"the outcome is unknown\" and loses that scope's acts until the first device deletes the row. If the reply is lost, that lock can last up to six minutes, with no way out before `notAfter` plus the margin. Say what the other device shows while a request is ordinarily in flight, and whether this lock follows the member or only the device that lost the reply. Add a case.\n\n4. Name the session's new authority. Today a session is a read credential: `holder` and `allows` check `claims.reads`. Under J1 the same token also deletes a row and reads the bytes of an unlanded request, using `holder(reader, \"summary\")`. A holder of that token can't forge a copy, because `POST` needs the signature. But it can read and deliver the member's pending acts and drop their buttons. Add a `ReadName`, or state why `summary` is enough. List who holds a person's session today, including `packages/mcp/src/views/member-reads.ts`. Your section 4 table has no row for a session holder, so add one.\n\n5. State the phone's clock. Section 5 sends the bytes at `notAfter` plus the margin by the second device's clock. If that clock runs ahead of the scope's by more than one minute, the act can land when Ana expected a refusal, bound by its signed revisions. S6's documents say this for one server, and J1 adds a second device's clock, so it needs the same sentence.\n\n6. Set the `POST` rule for a repeat. The row is keyed by digest, and only the signed intent is covered by it. `texts`, `presented` and `values` travel unsigned. Say that a second `POST` with the same digest and different bytes is refused and keeps the first, and add a case. Also say that these routes answer only on a membership scope.\n\n## Your six points\n\n1. Yes, that meets \"no new signature is made\". The session signature happens with or without J1, and its domain keeps it apart from every intent. Your two exact claims are the right ones to witness. I read `worker.ts`: `settle` and `acts` read no reader, as you say.\n\n2. Yes, within its row, the table holds. A held copy can only finish its signer's act. See item 4 for who can hold one, and item 5 for when. A delete loses only the button, and the journal stays true.\n\n3. Yes, the `device-key` invitation is inside J1. J1's witness needs a person's real second key, and the page can't make one. Keep it a separate tag with a closed input, as `checker-key` is. An admin-only route is a real limit for a person with no admin, and you state it.\n\n4. This is the planner's to rule. The request names the gap in three places, and local MCP custody is one of them. I accept page first, because the routes take any session and a later writer needs no new design. But page-only doesn't meet \"every person's act\", and J1's journey surface is the AI-chat surface as much as the page. So the note shouldn't read as closing the plan's condition. Name an owner for the `dap mcp` writer, or get a ruling that drops it.\n\n5. Yes, keep the copy first. An act sent without a copy is exactly the case J1 exists to end. `README.md:84` supports your claim that a granted act already reads this object, so availability adds little to those acts. Two things to carry into the build:\n   - Measure the two added requests in the evidence, by round trips under this Worker's local test or better. \"Nobody has measured\" is fine for a design and not for the landing.\n   - During a deployment's turnover, every page act fails for some minutes with \"nothing was sent\". State that to the planner as a deployment cost.\n\n6. I accept the caps and the seven days, with item 2. 64 rows per member and a 1 MiB body are ordinary for this room. Every `POST` is signed by the session's own key, so a member's use of the object is bounded and attributable.\n\n## Checks\n\nI read the note whole and these citations at b686862e: `worker.ts` around the act and settle routes, `core.ts` `settle`, `sessions.ts` `holder` and `SessionRequests`, `membership.ts` `invite-key`, and the role table. They match, apart from what's above. The kernel claim in section 7 reads right to me, since nothing enters the contract and the table sits outside the fold. I didn't read every other citation. `git diff` shows one file, the note. I ran nothing, and no independent second read ran.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved in substance. No new class, no migration, no kernel change, and it reuses S6's route.\n- Security: not yet. Items 1, 2 and 4 change what the note promises about who reads a copy and for how long.\n- Simplification: approved. No new state. I'd keep it that way when you answer item 3.\n\nThe build may start once the corrected note is approved. This review approves no code.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f9cb6bd5dc6da4042aa98ef3ef544ef26ca1d457","binding":"assigned","head":"40256d33458163b32a0a930992680f976ff45f05","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ab742c407f614158d62cb91ca52f982e7af59122\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3649cf4ac25e995f657730259cbc8b1b415812d9\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ab742c407f614158d62cb91ca52f982e7af59122","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}